/*!
\ingroup qmllint-warnings-and-errors

\title Structured value coercion
\brief [structured-value-coercion] A member of an object literal is coerced to the type of the
property of the structured value it becomes.

\qmllintwarningcategory structured-value-coercion

\section1 Structured value coercion

This warning category is disabled by default.

\section2 What happened?
An object literal was assigned to a property of a structured value type, passed as such an
argument, or returned as such a value, and one of its members is a string, a boolean, or a number
where the property of the value type is another one of these. Or it is a number written with a
fraction where the property is an integer.

\section2 Why is that bad?
JavaScript converts between strings, booleans and numbers without complaining, and the result
can be surprising. Any string that is not empty is \c true, including \c{"false"}. A number
loses its fraction when it becomes an integer.

\section2 Example
\qml
import QtQuick
import MyModule

Item {
    // myStructuredType has "property int number" and "property bool truth"
    property myStructuredType value: ({
        number: "42",    // becomes 42
        truth: "false"   // becomes true
    })
}
\endqml

To fix this warning, write the values with the types of the properties:

\qml
import QtQuick
import MyModule

Item {
    property myStructuredType value: ({
        number: 42,
        truth: false
    })
}
\endqml
*/
